hosen passwords has often been characterized using the information theory) Tj T* (concept of entropy [Shannon]. While entropy can be readily calculated for data having) Tj T* (deterministic distribution functions, estimating the entropy for user-chosen passwords is) Tj T* (difficult and past efforts to do so have not been particularly accurate. For this reason, a) Tj T* (different and somewhat simpler approach, based primarily on password length, is presented) Tj T* (herein.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Many attacks associated with the use of passwords are not affected by password complexity and) Tj T* (length. Keystroke logging, phishing, and social engineering attacks are equally effective on) Tj T* (lengthy, complex passwords as simple ones. These attacks are outside the scope of this Appendix.) Tj T* () Tj T* (A.2 Length) Tj T* () Tj T* (Password length has been found to be a primary factor in characterizing password strength) Tj T* ([Strength] [Composition]. Passwords that are too short yield to brute force attacks as well as) Tj T* (to dictionary attacks using words and commonly chosen passwords.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The minimum password length that should be required depends to a large extent on the threat) Tj T* (model being addressed. Online attacks where the attacker attempts to log in by guessing the) Tj T* (password can be mitigated by limiting the rate of login attempts permitted. In order to prevent) Tj T* (an attacker \(or a persistent claimant with poor typing skills\) from easily inflicting a denial-) Tj T* (of-service attack on the subscriber by making many incorrect guesses, passwords need to be) Tj T* (complex enough that rate limiting does not occur after a modest number of erroneous attempts,) Tj T* (but does occur before there is a significant chance of a successful guess.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Page 27 of 29) Tj T* ET
