ices. Many services reject passwords with spaces and various special characters. In some) Tj T* (cases, the special characters that are not accepted might be an effort to avoid attacks like SQL) Tj T* (injection that depend on those characters. But a properly hashed password would not be sent) Tj T* (intact to a database in any case, so such precautions are unnecessary. Users should also be able) Tj T* (to include space characters to allow the use of phrases. Spaces themselves, however, add little) Tj T* (to the complexity of passwords and may introduce usability issues \(e.g., the undetected use of) Tj T* (two spaces rather than one\), so it may be beneficial to remove repeated spaces in typed) Tj T* (passwords prior to verification.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Users' password choices are very predictable, so attackers are likely to guess passwords that) Tj T* (have been successful in the past. These include dictionary words and passwords from previous) Tj T* (breaches, such as the "Password1!" example above. For this reason, it is recommended that) Tj T* (passwords chosen by users be compared against a "black list" of unacceptable passwords. This) Tj T* (list should include passwords from previous breach corpuses, dictionary words, and specific) Tj T* (words \(such as the name of the service itself\) that users are likely to choose. Since user) Tj T* (choice of passwords will also be governed by a minimum length requirement, this dictionary need) Tj T* (only include entries meeting that requirement.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Highly complex memorized secrets introduce a new potential vulnerability: they are less likely) Tj T* (to be memorable, and it is more likely that they will be written down or stored electronically) Tj T* (in an unsafe manner. While these practices are not necessarily vulnerable, statistically some) Tj T* (methods of recording such secrets will be. This is an additional motivation not to require) Tj T* (excessively long or complex memorized secrets.) Tj T* () Tj 